# Normalize line endings on the way in, check out LF everywhere. The compiler's lexer and the
# fixtures' expected output are byte-exact, and `scripts/corpus.manifest` hashes captured streams —
# a CRLF checkout on another platform would move rows for reasons that have nothing to do with code.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.md diff=markdown

# The committed bootstrap seed is a static ELF. `text=auto` would guess right, but guessing is not a
# contract: an accidental EOL conversion here would corrupt the one artifact the whole trust chain
# rests on.
seed/alatyr binary

# Generated, and large. `scripts/corpus.manifest` is a 6600-row behaviour oracle: a legitimate change
# to it is a handful of rows, and a reviewer must be able to see those rows without scrolling past the
# whole file. `-diff` collapses it in review; `linguist-generated` keeps it out of language stats.
#
# `-merge` is the load-bearing one, and it is not cosmetic. Both files are line-oriented, so git's
# default text merge will combine two branches that regenerated DISJOINT row sets without reporting a
# conflict — yielding a manifest that is the output of NEITHER compiler, and whose next `--check` on
# main fails with a diff that reads like a regression in whichever side landed second. With `merge`
# unset git refuses instead: it takes the single side cleanly when only one side changed the file, and
# declares a conflict when both did. That is exactly the ownership rule the protocol used to ask
# people to keep by hand.
scripts/corpus.manifest -diff -merge linguist-generated
scripts/idiom.baseline   -diff -merge linguist-generated

# The THIRD oracle, and it is deliberately NOT `-diff`. `scripts/needle.baseline` is ~40 reviewed
# lines, not 6600 generated ones: collapsing its diff would hide exactly the change a reviewer must
# see (a lane grandfathering a 21st vacuous assertion). It needs `-merge` for the same reason as the
# other two — it is line-oriented, so two branches repaying DISJOINT debt would merge clean into a
# baseline that matches neither run of the gate. `scripts/land.sh` already treats all three as
# oracles; this line is what makes git agree.
scripts/needle.baseline  -merge

# `scripts/axis.baseline` is line-oriented and reviewed, so it needs `-merge` for the same reason the
# three oracles do: two branches recording DISJOINT reviewed cells would merge clean into a file that
# matches neither run of the detector. It is deliberately NOT being called a fourth oracle here — that
# word carries `scripts/land.sh`'s enforcement and the one-oracle-per-PR rule, and the protocol's list
# lives in AGENTS.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, the PR template, land.sh and three skills. `scripts/axis_gate.sh`
# is not wired into `full.sh`, so this file gates nothing yet; promoting it is a maintainer decision and
# a separate change. This line is the part that is safe and useful today.
scripts/axis.baseline -merge

# The PREDICTED DELTA a behaviour-changing feature PR declares (scripts/corpus_manifest.sh,
# "THE PREDICTED DELTA"). Deliberately NOT `-diff`: its rows are the transitions the reviewer must
# read in the feature PR, and they are a handful where the manifest is thousands. `-merge` for the
# oracles' reason — two branches declaring DISJOINT rows must not merge clean into a prediction
# neither of them made; it is not an oracle, and land.sh does not treat it as one.
scripts/corpus.expected-delta -merge

# The KNOWN DEVIATIONS (#1126 S2) are `merge=union`, the opposite of the oracles above, and the
# difference is what the gate checks. Each behaviour fix deletes its OWN rows, so independent fixes
# touch disjoint lines and a union merge keeps both deletions. When two deletions sit in one hunk,
# union keeps BOTH sides' lines: the rows come back. A manifest merged that way would be wrong and
# pass, which is why the manifest is `-merge`. A deviation row that comes back is caught: it no longer
# reproduces (STALE), and the restored lines are out of sort order. `fixture_expect.sh --gate` fails
# on both, on the merged object, before it can land.
scripts/known-deviations.tsv merge=union
